## METHOD BEING FOLLOWED (proven across all platforms so far)

1. Understand content supply first (audit real content) before platform
   strategy.
2. Lock order: **content pool → format → volume/cadence → platform-specific
   mechanics → wording/output format** — never start with caption wording.
3. Research each platform's actual current safe volume/frequency and
   algorithm behavior before fixing any daily number — never guess, never
   reuse one platform's numbers for another without fresh research (each
   platform's norms differ significantly — proven true every time so far:
   Facebook 3x/day, Instagram 1x/day, X 4x/day, YouTube 3x/day, all
   different).
4. Verify any generated/sorted data file programmatically (counts, dupes,
   gaps) before presenting it as final — use bash_tool, never eyeball large
   lists.
5. When a research finding contradicts something already assumed or stated,
   correct it plainly and immediately — don't bury the correction.
6. Site is blocked for direct AI fetches (web_fetch on mirelleinspo.com URLs
   fails with PERMISSIONS_ERROR unless the URL surfaced via search first).
   Workaround: `site:mirelleinspo.com [exact-slug]` via web_search often
   surfaces the real page or close related pages. This is inconsistent —
   some pages index, some don't (appears tied to how new/low-authority the
   page is). When the exact slug doesn't surface, the fallback is: infer
   the topic from the slug name, then research it properly on the open web
   (not generic knowledge) — same rigor either way, confirmed explicitly by
   operator as the acceptable fallback method.

